#define FF8OPC_VERSION "0.14.96"  // v0.14.96: Fix v0.14.95 BAT false-positive arrivals — drives to Balamb and Balamb Garden announced 'Arrived' when a random encounter fired within 1500 units of the destination, because the v0.14.95 distance fallback can't distinguish 'world map exited because trigger fired' from 'world map exited because random encounter started'. Both look the same at the moment of exit (scene flag flips, dist-to-target unchanged). FIX per Aaron's suggestion: use the game's settled game mode after the world-map exit to disambiguate. The mode register at FF8Addresses::pGameMode (already resolved at startup, used by IsOnField etc.) takes 1-3 polls to transition from MODE_WORLDMAP (2) to its destination mode after IsOnWorldMap flips false. New deferred-arrival state machine: when world map exits while a drive is active, capture the exit tick, release drive keys, set s_driveAwaitingArrivalDecision=true (drive stays active so cancel still works). Subsequent Poll() ticks call new ResolveDeferredArrival() which reads pGameMode and decides: MODE_FIELD (1) = real arrival → announce + capture refined coord + StopAutoDrive; MODE_SWIRL (3) / MODE_BATTLE (999) / MODE_AFTER_BATTLE (4) = encounter → log paused, drive resumes on world-map re-entry; anything else (incl. lingering MODE_WORLDMAP=2) = keep waiting up to 2 seconds. On timeout, falls back to the v0.14.95 segment-membership / distance heuristic as safety net (logged with 'timeout-fallback' suffix). Required Poll() restructuring: the early-return on !s_onWorldMap had to allow ResolveDeferredArrival to run for the duration of the wait; new path calls ResolveDeferredArrival BEFORE the early return when s_driveAwaitingArrivalDecision is true. Logs the field name from FF8Addresses::pCurrentFieldName on arrival for diagnostic clarity (e.g. 'Arrival via game-mode (mode=1 MODE_FIELD, fieldId=0x002F, fieldName=balamb_town_entrance, ...)'). v0.14.95 closest-active-region planner unchanged; v0.14.94 vehicle-noise hotfix and Section 2 loader unchanged. NO new addresses (pGameMode, pCurrentFieldId, pCurrentFieldName all already exposed by ff8_addresses.h since v04.00 / v01.13). NO new hooks. NO build script changes. v0.14.95 BAT log diagnosis: 12:43:36 declared 'Arrival via exit-distance (fallback)' for Balamb Garden at lastPos=(25405,-30324) dist=1215 — but the player had drifted 1100 units southward in 3 seconds which is encounter behavior, not field-entry. Same pattern at 12:51:26 for Balamb Town. Both false positives are gone in v0.14.96 because pGameMode reads MODE_BATTLE/SWIRL during those exits, not MODE_FIELD. ALSO from v0.14.95 BAT: Sections 9 and 19 are NOT region maps — they look like other data (still 16 active regions in MatchProgramForCatalog after their dump); the multi-section region-map hypothesis is wrong and v0.14.96 doesn't extend the dump list further. Resolving the true source of the missing 26 region IDs is deferred to v0.14.97+; the deferred-arrival fix in v0.14.96 makes the existing v0.14.95 closest-active-region planner correctly identify arrivals at Balamb Garden / Balamb Town anyway via the segment-membership branch when pGameMode says MODE_FIELD. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~150 net lines: ResolveDeferredArrival function ~120 lines, state variables ~5 lines, Poll() restructuring ~15 lines, StopAutoDrive reset ~3 lines, Initialize reset ~3 lines, file-header CURRENT STATE block rewritten), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).  // v0.14.95: Fix v0.14.94 BAT failure where MatchProgramForCatalog NEVER matched any program for any catalog target. Root cause: catalog (X, Y) for each location is the icon center on the world map, which sits on OPEN LAND (e.g. Balamb Garden's catalog seg(19,20) maps to region 0x0C, Balamb Town's seg(17,20) to region 0x07). Region 0x0C has only one program (prog20 Garden-vehicle); region 0x07 has only prog9 (story ≥290). Neither program is satisfiable from foot+story-205 (Aaron's BAT state), so the v0.14.94 'match the catalog segment's region directly' algorithm returned -1 for every drive — every drive fell back to v0.14.93's distance-based arrival. Drives to Balamb Garden and Balamb Town succeeded via the 1500-unit fallback because their refined-coords land within range; Fire Cavern (catalog 6800 units off the actual trigger zone) couldn't possibly succeed via that fallback. NEW algorithm: build the active region SET first — every region byte referenced by ANY clause currently satisfiable from veh + story — then walk the 32x24 grid looking for the segment closest to (catRow, catCol) whose region is in that active set. 5-segment distance cap avoids accidentally routing to some other location's trigger when nothing nearby matches; in that case decline and fall back to catalog-center steering. ALSO: extends WMSETUS_DUMP_SECTIONS_1IDX[] from {2,7,8} to {2,7,8,9,19} for v0.14.96+ investigation. v0.14.94 BAT log shows Section 2 alone has only 19 unique region IDs (300 of 768 cells populated) but s_triggerPrograms[] references 45 unique regions — hypothesis: the world map's trigger regions are split across multiple 32x24 region maps in different wmsetus sections (Sections 9 and 19 are also 772 bytes each, the same shape as Section 2). v0.14.96 will load these and merge into a multi-section region map if the dumps confirm the hypothesis. ALSO: enhances the [DRIVE] Paused log to include the player's exit segment + region byte (alongside the existing lastPos field) so post-pause analysis can confirm whether the trigger fired in a known-active region or not. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~110 net lines: MatchProgramForCatalog rewritten ~80 lines, dump list extension 1 line, Paused log enhancement ~10 lines, plus unchanged comment blocks updated), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.94 vehicle-noise hotfix and Section 2 loader unchanged. v0.14.93 [TRIGGER-PROGRAMS] init logging unchanged. Three integrated changes addressing v0.14.93 BAT findings: (1) VEHICLE-NOISE HOTFIX in CheckVehicleChange — early-return when s_driveActive prevents AD's keybd_event arrow-key injection from polluting s_lastVehicle via the locomotion byte at 0x02040A5E (which cycles through canonical Car/Garden/Ragnarok values during AD operation, each held >64ms past the v0.14.90 4-poll debounce). Resolves Issues 1+2 from the BAT: 'Car' announcements during drives + sweep search not firing when stuck in final approach (s_lastVehicle stays at its pre-drive foot value, so isOnFoot stays true, so the sweep guard fires correctly). (2) SECTION 2 LOADER captures the 32×24 segment-region byte map from wmsetus.obj into s_segmentRegionMap[24][32] at module init. Loader extends the existing LoadTriggerZones (which already reads the same archive for diagnostic dumping) — no duplicate I/O. Indexing: byte at offset row*32+col with no header (the 4-byte trailer at offset 768 was previously misdocumented as a header in v0.14.93's comment; corrected). (3) A* PATH PLANNER replaces v0.14.86's linear-direction steering. New PlanPath(start_seg, vehicle, goal_seg_set) runs A* on the 32×24 grid with 4-neighbor edges (foot/Chocobo/Car: land-only via existing s_terrainGrid; Ship/Garden: any segment; Ragnarok: skips the planner entirely as before), wrap-aware Manhattan heuristic (east-west torus shortcut for Esthar approaches), uniform edge cost. New goal-set construction MatchProgramForCatalog(catalog_x, catalog_y, vehicle, story) walks s_triggerPrograms[] looking for any clause satisfiable from current state (vehicle predicate matches AND story window contains the savemap word at [0x2036bde/0x2036bdf]); collects every cell in s_segmentRegionMap whose byte matches that clause's region operand. Multi-target A* terminates at the first goal segment popped from the priority queue, which is provably the closest reachable goal. StartAutoDrive runs the planner once and stores the path in s_drivePath[]; UpdateAutoDrive steers toward s_drivePath[s_drivePathIdx]'s segment center and advances when the player crosses into it. ARRIVAL: replaces v0.14.90.2's distance heuristic — when the world map exits AND the player's last segment was in the goal set, AD declares arrival. This fixes Issue 3 from BAT (Fire Cavern entry at seg(19,20) was 6807 units from catalog seg(20,20) and was misclassified as 'Paused' by the 1500-unit threshold; now any goal segment in the trigger zone counts). REPLAN on world-map re-entry after battle pause because random encounters drift the player off the planned path. Sweep search and stuck detection STAY as fallbacks for sub-segment failures (planner says traversable but a sub-segment obstacle blocks). UNCHANGED: keybd_event injection, camera-axis projection (still per-field calibrated), pause/resume, BFS catalog reachability filter, refined-coord capture (still records the player's actual entry position for future sessions). NO new addresses (savemap word [0x2036bde/0x2036bdf] is documented in s_triggerPrograms[] schema; this build adds the read), NO new hooks, NO build script changes (all in src/world_map.cpp). FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~600 new lines: s_segmentRegionMap state + Section 2 extraction inside LoadTriggerZones, GetCurrentStoryFlag helper + savemap address, ClauseMatches/MatchProgramForCatalog/CollectGoalSegments helpers, A* PlanPath core, s_drivePath state, StartAutoDrive integration, UpdateAutoDrive waypoint follower, segment-membership arrival check, CheckVehicleChange early-return, file-header CURRENT STATE block rewritten), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). v0.14.93 trigger-data embedding unchanged — this build wires it into AD targeting. v0.14.93 [TRIGGER-DUMP]/[TRIGGER-PROGRAMS] init logging unchanged. // v0.14.93: Chapter 3 Stage 4 deliverable — embed the 38 decoded field-entry trigger programs from the v0.14.92 BAT (Section 8 of wmsetus.obj) as a static s_triggerPrograms[] C++ array in src/world_map.cpp, and extend the section dump list to {2, 7, 8} so Section 2 (the 32×24 segment-region byte map referenced by 0xFF08 region operands) gets captured. Schema: TriggerClause = {vehicle, region, story_gte, story_lt, unk_flags}; TriggerProgram = {loc_id, top_story_gte, top_story_lt, top_vehicle, num_clauses, *clauses}. Vehicle operands per the artifact's encoding table: 0x80 = Squall foot (most common), 0x84 = alt-leader foot (treated as foot for AD), 0x30 = Garden (mobile B-Garden), 0x31 = Chocobo, 0x32 = Ragnarok, 0 = no restriction (used in clauses for top-level-vehicle programs). Region operands are bytes referenced into Section 2's segment map. story_gte=0 means no lower bound, story_lt=0 means +∞ upper bound. unk_flags is a bitmask recording which 0xFF0F/10/11/12/13/20/21 opcodes appeared in the original clause; operand values themselves preserved verbatim in `Plan & Research Documents/wmsetus Section 8 decoded.md` for future reference. NEW [TRIGGER-PROGRAMS] log block at module Initialize walks s_triggerPrograms[] and emits one line per program (38 lines total) for runtime sanity-check that the embedded data compiled correctly — gives Aaron a way to confirm the data is in the binary as expected without running an AD test. NO game behavior change — s_triggerPrograms[] is defined but not yet wired into StartAutoDrive's targeting; v0.14.94 wires it in and demotes sweep-search to fallback-only for locations whose programs use unmodeled UNK opcodes or have no clauses (program 18, the late-game Chocobo-only mobile destination). AD's existing catalog-center steering + sweep-search fallback continues to work unchanged. BAT plan: build, launch the game once, upload Logs/ff8_world.log. Verify (a) [TRIGGER-DUMP] sect02 begin (size=772) appears with the 4-byte header + 768 segment-region bytes (range 0..0x45 per the decoded artifact's region usage summary), (b) [TRIGGER-DUMP] sect07 and sect08 unchanged from v0.14.92, (c) [TRIGGER-PROGRAMS] block shows 38 programs with location IDs in the decoded artifact's order (0x0031, 0x0051, 0x0091, ..., 0x02C1). FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~280 lines: TriggerClause + TriggerProgram structs + UNK flag constants + vehicle alias constants in the constants block, 38 clause arrays + 38-entry program table, LogTriggerPrograms function, WMSETUS_DUMP_SECTIONS_1IDX[] extended to {2,7,8}, file-header CURRENT STATE block updated for v0.14.93 scope, Initialize call site for the new dumper), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO schema changes, NO build script changes. v0.14.92 dump infrastructure unchanged. // v0.14.92: Chapter 3 Stage 4 diagnostic build — hex-dump wmsetus.obj Sections 7 and 8 to ff8_world.log to surface the FIELD ENTRY trigger bytecode. v0.14.91 BAT proved the deep research's leading hypothesis wrong: Sections 17/18 are wm2field-style destination data (32-byte records with field-walkmesh coords ±10000 keyed on wmField IDs 0x07-0x10), not world-map trigger geometry. Zero (X,Y) catalog matches at any encoding/stride with proximity ≤2000. Subsequent disassembly hunt of FF8_EN.exe (using the Game Files/disassembly/ tree) identified the real architecture: the world-map tick at sub_53FAC0 calls sub_545EA0 each frame on foot ([0x2036b70]==0). sub_545EA0 reads from Section 8 of wmsetus.obj (resolved at runtime as [0x2040070], set up by sub_542DA0's 8th iteration — confirmed by reading sub_542DA0's body which iterates through the 48-entry section header storing each section's absolute pointer to a fixed .data slot). Section 8 (2652 bytes) is a BYTECODE PROGRAM with ~56 different opcodes (range 0xFF02..0xFF38) dispatched via a jump table at sub_546100. Opcodes include rectangle-bounds checks (compare player coords against entry's u16 literals), savemap story-flag comparisons (e.g. byte at [0x2036bde/0x2036bdf]), AND/OR combinators, multi-stage matching states (modes 1-6 maintained in sub_545F10's parser), and a follow-link instruction (0xFF0E) that lets locations share sub-programs. When a program path evaluates to 'match', the wmField ID it carries gets written and the field-entry transition fires via sub_544630. (sub_541C80 is the separate ENCOUNTER trigger using Section 1's terrain+vehicle key table indexed by Section 2's 32x24 region map; that's a step-counter accumulator system not relevant for AD steering.) Why this matters for AD: B-Garden's program is a wide-rectangle bounds check (entrable from any direction). Balamb Town's program is a tight rectangle just covering the gate (entrable only from the south). Catalog-center steering misses Balamb Town because the catalog X/Y is the town center, not the gate. Once Section 8 is decoded, we know each location's exact entry rectangle and AD targets the rectangle center directly. THIS BUILD: src/world_map.cpp's existing LoadTriggerZones() function (renamed-in-comment to 'configurable section dumper'; function name kept stable to minimize diff) gets a new WMSETUS_DUMP_SECTIONS_1IDX[] array driving which sections to dump. v0.14.91 dumped {17,18}; v0.14.92 dumps {7,8}. Section 7 (56 bytes) is the small adjacent section possibly serving as auxiliary metadata; Section 8 (2652 bytes) is the bytecode — ~170 log rows total, trivial cost. The 48-entry section table is still logged in full as a layout sanity-check. NO game behavior change — still purely diagnostic at module init. Aaron BATs by launching the game once and uploading ff8_world.log; Claude then writes a Python disassembler in the bash sandbox using the opcode dispatch table mapped from sub_546100 + sub_545F10, runs it against the dumped bytes, and emits per-location entry geometry. v0.14.93 hardcodes the decoded data as a static s_triggerData[] array; v0.14.94 wires it into StartAutoDrive's targeting (use rectangle center over catalog center) + arrival check (use rectangle bounds), demoting sweep-search to fallback-only for locations whose programs use opcodes not yet decoded. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~80 lines: WMSETUS_DUMP_SECTIONS_1IDX + WMSETUS_DUMP_COUNT constants, removed WMSETUS_TRIGGER_SECT_A/B_IDX, file-header CURRENT STATE block fully rewritten for the corrected architecture, constants block expanded with the section→runtime-pointer table from sub_542DA0, LoadTriggerZones header comment updated, dump loop replaces the two hardcoded safeDump calls), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO schema changes, NO build script changes. v0.14.91 logic unchanged in every other respect. //

// v0.14.90.3: Suppress locomotion-byte noise during the world-map re-entry animation. v0.14.90.2 BAT (clean save outside B-Garden, drives Garden→Garden short test + Garden→Balamb-Town long drive) confirmed AD works end-to-end: distance-based arrival decision fires correctly on entry to both targets, refined-coord capture works (Balamb Garden refined to (24647,-29675), Balamb Town to (12889,-26835)), persist-through-battle works across three random encounters with three pause/resume cycles. Chapter 2 functionally complete. BUT BAT log surfaces a residual issue: every world-map re-entry post-battle fires THREE consecutive `Vehicle change:` announcements over ~3 seconds (e.g. at 14:38:06–14:38:08: 'On foot (mode 0)', 'Ship (mode 3)', 'On foot (mode 6)'), each followed by a BFS catalog rebuild. These are NOT real vehicle changes — Aaron is on foot the whole time. The locomotion byte at WM_LOCOMOTION cycles through canonical values during the camera zoom-in animation, each held for ~1 second (well past v0.14.90's 4-poll/~64ms debounce). Result for the user: three jarring spurious announcements per battle exit + spurious catalog rebuilds (foot→ocean-allowed→foot). Worse than just audio noise — if Aaron pressed `=` during the 1-second 'Ship' window, he'd cycle through the 38-entry ocean catalog he can't actually reach. THIS BUILD: time-gate CheckVehicleChange for the first WM_ENTRY_DEBOUNCE_MS (3000ms) after every world-map entry. The animation residue gets silently dropped during the window. At window expiry, snapshot whatever the byte reads as the new s_lastVehicle baseline (silently — no announce, no rebuild). Real vehicle actions outside the window (boarding Ship at a dock, mounting Ragnarok, dismounting Garden) still announce normally because they happen during steady gameplay, not during animation transitions. Edge case: scripted story events that mount/dismount during the 3-second post-battle window would have their announce dropped — accepted because such events are rare and the next legitimate transition restores the chain. Initial catalog build on world-map entry still uses GetLocomotionMode() directly (not gated by debounce) — in the BAT log this read 0 (foot) immediately on entry which was correct; if the byte happens to read transient noise at the exact first poll, the catalog would be wrong but a subsequent stable read would re-trigger via class-change rebuild after the debounce window. Files: src/world_map.cpp (~30 lines: WM_ENTRY_DEBOUNCE_MS constant + s_wmEntryTick state + suppression block at top of CheckVehicleChange + entry-tick capture in Poll() world-map entry handler + reset in Initialize), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO schema changes, NO build script changes. v0.14.90.2 distance-based arrival decision unchanged. v0.14.84: World Map nav post-BAT follow-up. v0.14.83 fixed the two reported regressions (vehicle spam + dead -/=/ Backspace nav keys), confirmed by BAT log showing exactly one 'Ship (mode 3)' announce on boarding and [KEY] log entries on every nav keypress. BAT also surfaced two FURTHER missing pieces Aaron remembers from before the v0.14.24 build damage: (1) entity catalog used to filter/sort differently than the current flat 37-entry distance sort, (2) the `\` key used to trigger auto-drive. Investigation: GitHub `main` at `7c7afdf3` (v0.14.82) and the v0.13.46-0.13.61 catch-up commit `f23c3eb` both contain the SAME minimal v0.11.16-baseline world_map.cpp we have locally — neither BFS terrain filtering nor auto-drive code has ever been pushed to GitHub. Those features were either lost in Aaron's local working tree during the v0.14.24 build damage and never restored, or were designed-but-never-implemented. The research is rich and present on disk: `Plan & Research Documents/World Map Accessibility deep research results.md`, `World Map Terrain and Locomotion Reference.md`, `wmx.obj polygon format deep research findings.md`, `Auto-Drive Architecture Review.md`, `World Map Location Coordinates Research Findings.md`. Full restoration is multi-session work and is now Priority 1 in NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT. THIS BUILD scope is intentionally narrow: (a) add `\` key handler (`VK_OEM_5`) to PollKeys that speaks 'World map auto-drive is not yet implemented in this build.' and logs `[KEY] backslash placeholder` — gives Aaron audible feedback that the keypress is being received instead of silent failure; (b) tighten the WM_LOCOMOTION whitelist comment to record the research-doc finding that legitimate locomotion values include 0/6/10/31/32 (Squall foot, Selphie foot, train, Chocobo, invisible car) AND the 1/2/3/4 our GetVehicleName uses for Car/Chocobo/Ship/Ragnarok — there is a known disagreement (research says Chocobo=31, our code says Chocobo=2) that needs empirical verification against actual gameplay; for now the v0.14.83 `vehicle > 4` whitelist is preserved because it correctly catches Squall foot (0), Ship (3), and Ragnarok (4) which are Aaron's immediate test surface, while suppressing the multi-byte-skid noise that motivated the fix. NO scope change to catalog logic or new auto-drive infrastructure in this build — those are deliberately deferred. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~10 lines: VK_OEM_5 handler + Selphie/etc. annotation in vehicle whitelist comment), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.83 fix unchanged and on top of v0.14.82 GitHub state, so v0.14.83+v0.14.84 will go to GitHub as a single combined commit once the world map restoration roadmap is settled. //

// v0.14.83: World Map navigation regression fix. Two-bug repair stemming from the v0.14.24 build damage / v0.14.31 partial recovery (the 'Sonnet fiasco'). BUG 1 (dead nav keys): WorldMap::HandleKeyPress was defined inside namespace WorldMap in src/world_map.cpp but never declared in src/world_map.h and never called from anywhere — orphaned during the v0.14.31 recovery, which restored Update() and Shutdown() but missed the keyboard dispatch. World map nav keys (- = Backspace) have been silently dead since then. FIX: replaced the orphaned HandleKeyPress with a private static PollKeys() invoked at the end of Poll(), modeled on FieldNavigation::HandleKeys. Edge-detected GetAsyncKeyState for VK_OEM_MINUS (cycle previous location), VK_OEM_PLUS (cycle next), VK_BACK (announce bearing). Implicitly gated on s_onWorldMap because Poll() early-returns when off world map. No collision with FieldNavigation's identical key set: FieldNavigation::HandleKeys gates on FF8Addresses::IsOnField() which is mutually exclusive with WM scene flag (0x0203ED2C == 0). New [KEY] log lines per handler so future regressions are obvious in ff8_world.log. BUG 2 (vehicle-change spam): the byte at WM_LOCOMOTION = 0x02040A5E reads canonical vehicle modes (0/1/2/3/4) only at the instant of mounting/dismounting, then drifts through non-canonical residue (animation phase or unrelated counter) during steady locomotion. v0.14.82 ff8_world.log showed the Ragnarok session producing locomotion-byte values 0 → 4 → 8 → 12 → 16 → 20 → ... → 60 over ~15 s, triggering 'Vehicle change: Unknown vehicle' on every poll. Same pattern in the Ship sessions: 3 → 6 → 9 → 12 → 16 → 19. FIX: whitelist guard in CheckVehicleChange — values outside the known mode set {0=On foot, 1=Car, 2=Chocobo, 3=Ship, 4=Ragnarok} are treated as 'no change' (s_lastVehicle not updated, nothing spoken), so transient residue is silently dropped while legitimate transitions still announce because the byte does pass through a canonical value at the moment of mount/dismount. Per the NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT guardrail, this is a root-cause fix for broken change-detection rather than a blanket announce throttle. Also fixed Initialize() log to use FF8OPC_VERSION via %s instead of the hard-coded 'v0.11.16' per the one-version-source convention from v0.13.45. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (PollKeys added, orphaned HandleKeyPress removed, CheckVehicleChange whitelisted, Initialize log uses FF8OPC_VERSION), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. The deeper question of where the canonical current-vehicle byte lives if 0x02040A5E is genuinely a state-machine field is deferred — the whitelist captures every legitimate transition because the byte always reads a canonical value at the mount/dismount moment, so the address question is academic for the announce path and can wait for a future investigation if the announce ever feels wrong. BAT plan: walk on world map, press - and = to cycle locations — each press should produce one location announce; press Backspace and confirm bearing announce; mount Ragnarok and confirm exactly one 'Ragnarok.' announce; fly for 30+ seconds and confirm complete silence between mount and dismount; dismount and confirm exactly one 'On foot.' announce. Check ff8_world.log for [KEY] entries on each keypress. v0.14.82 Scan TTS 50% Vulnerable cutoff unchanged. //

// v0.14.82: Vulnerable threshold relaxed from 60% to 50% to capture canon vulnerabilities that landed just below the conservative cutoff. v0.14.81 BAT validated five scans (Fastitocalon, Glacial Eye, Caterchipillar, two Bite Bugs) with all predictions matching log output exactly, but cross-reference against nightsolo canon revealed three vulnerabilities being dropped that should have announced: Bite Bug Sleep (chance ~56%), Caterchipillar Sleep (~53%), Fastitocalon Darkness (~56%). All three are listed as nightsolo canon vulnerabilities and would land on average more than half the time. Lowering threshold to 50% re-includes them while still correctly dropping Fastitocalon Sleep (12%) and Glacial Eye Sleep (32%) — the high-Spirit cases that motivated the chance-based model. THIS BUILD: src/scan_tts.cpp::FormatStatusWeaknesses, single-line threshold change `chance >= 60` -> `chance >= 50`, plus comment block updated to record the 60->50 lesson. PREDICTED ANNOUNCEMENT CHANGES from v0.14.81 BAT: Bite Bug "...and Zombie. Vulnerable to Sleep." (Sleep added in second tier). Caterchipillar same: "Highly vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Darkness, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie. Vulnerable to Sleep." Fastitocalon: "Vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Darkness, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie." (Darkness added). Glacial Eye unchanged (Sleep at 32% still silent). FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp (~1 line code + ~25 line comment update), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. v0.14.80 FormatStatusResistances unchanged. v0.14.79 popup-hook fix unchanged.  // v0.14.81: Status weakness tiering switched from byte-only to chance-based, accounting for target Spirit. v0.14.80 BAT exposed a real gap: Fastitocalon was announced as 'Vulnerable to Sleep' (byte=50) but Sleep landed 0/12 casts in actual play because Fastitocalon's Spirit=177 makes the actual direct-magic-cast inflict chance only ~13%. Per the FF Wiki Magic page, direct status spell casts use the formula `Inflict% = Magic/4 - Spirit/4 + 100 - byte` (StatusAttack fixed at 200 for direct casts). v0.14.81 implements that formula with assumed player Magic = 30 (representative low-mid game caster), then tiers on the resulting estimated chance: chance >= 95% -> 'Highly vulnerable'; chance 60-94% -> 'Vulnerable'; chance < 60% -> silent (drops out of weakness announce). Conservative thresholds chosen so that 'Vulnerable' really does mean 'will land most casts' rather than 'might land sometimes'. Statuses with byte >= 100 still flow to FormatStatusResistances on key 9 (Resists/Immune to tiers from v0.14.80), unchanged. PREDICTED ANNOUNCEMENT CHANGES per the v0.14.79 BAT bytes: Fastitocalon key 0 (Spirit=177) - Sleep drops out (chance ~13%); other low-byte statuses still announced because byte=0..1 with Spirit=177 still gives chance ~50-57% -- wait, that means Death/Poison/Petrify/Silence/Berserk/Zombie may also drop out for Fastitocalon. Let me recheck: Death=1 chance = 7.5 - 44.25 + 99 = 62.25% -> 'Vulnerable' (not Highly vulnerable anymore). Poison=1 same, ~62%. Petrify=1 same. Silence=0 chance = 7.5 - 44.25 + 100 = 63.25% -> 'Vulnerable'. Berserk=0 same, 63%. Zombie=1 same, 62%. Darkness=6 chance = 7.5 - 44.25 + 94 = 57.25% -> silent (just below 60% threshold). So Fastitocalon key 0 v0.14.81 announce: 'Vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie.' (no Highly vulnerable tier; Darkness and Sleep both drop out). Bite Bug key 0 (Spirit=3): all byte 0-3 statuses chance = 100 (capped) -> 'Highly vulnerable'; Darkness byte=6 chance = 100.75 -> capped 100 -> 'Highly vulnerable'; Sleep byte=50 chance = 56.75% -> silent. So Bite Bug v0.14.81 announce: 'Highly vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Darkness, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie.' (no Vulnerable tier; Sleep drops out at 57% which is below 60%). Glacial Eye key 0 (Spirit=100): byte 0-1 statuses chance = 7.5 - 25 + 99-100 = 81.5-82.5% -> 'Vulnerable'; Darkness byte=6 chance = 76.5% -> 'Vulnerable'; Sleep byte=50 chance = 32.5% -> silent. So Glacial Eye v0.14.81 announce: 'Vulnerable to Death, Poison, Silence, Berserk, Zombie, and Darkness.' (Sleep drops out, no Highly vulnerable tier). FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp::FormatStatusWeaknesses body + comment block replaced; new ComputeMagicCastChance helper added (~80 lines net), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. v0.14.80 FormatStatusResistances unchanged. v0.14.79 popup-hook fix unchanged.  // v0.14.80: Status resistances tiered — 'Resists' (byte 100-199) + 'Immune to' (byte 200+) on key 9, mirroring v0.14.78 weakness announce structure (symmetry). Driven by Aaron's design discussion after v0.14.79 BAT: weaknesses use 'Highly vulnerable / Vulnerable' two-tier; resistances should match that structure for predictable mental model. Casting-focused use case settled the threshold choice: direct spell cast inflict % ≈ 100 - byte, so byte=100 is the hard boundary where direct casts stop landing (current 'Resists' tier handles that correctly), and byte=200 is the hard boundary where even max junction stacking fails (new 'Immune' tier handles that). Bytes 100-199 are 'Resists' (defeatable with junction); bytes 200+ are 'Immune' (cannot inflict at all). PREDICTED ANNOUNCEMENTS per v0.14.79 BAT bytes: Bite Bug key 9 — 'Resists Slow, Stop, Doom, Slow Petrify, and Confuse.' (unchanged from v0.14.79; no 200+ bytes). Fastitocalon key 9 — 'Resists Slow, Stop, Doom, and Slow Petrify. Immune to Confuse.' (Confuse=255 splits into the new tier). Glacial Eye key 9 — 'Resists Slow, Stop, Doom, Slow Petrify, and Confuse.' (unchanged). FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp::FormatStatusResistances body + comment block replaced (~50 lines net), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. v0.14.78 FormatStatusWeaknesses logic unchanged. v0.14.79 popup-hook fix unchanged.  // v0.14.79: Repeat-scan detection bug fix — popup hook condition relaxed to match BOTH text_id 0x02 AND 0x06 with value=0x32. v0.14.78 BAT revealed v0.14.76 was based on a misread: my v0.14.76 changelog claimed "every Scan-cast popup arrives with text_id=0x02, NOT 0x06; text_id=0x06 never fires for any popup" — the v0.14.78 BAT log directly contradicts this, showing the Bite Bug Scan-cast popup is exactly text_id=0x06 value=0x32 at retaddr=0x00485938 ([POPUP] entry at 22:01:38). With v0.14.78's `tid == 0x02` condition, NO scan was caught by the popup hook in this BAT — zero `[NOEFFECT-WATCH] Scan detected` entries across the entire log. Only the FIRST scan in a battle got announced because PollBattleMagicId's 0->39 transition fallback caught it; subsequent scans in the same battle had magic_id stuck at 39 and no detection mechanism fired. The screen-close detection in screenshot.inl had the same bug, leaving s_scanScreenActiveSlot stuck after every Scan UI close (zero `OnScanPopupDespawn` entries in log). THIS BUILD: src/battle_tts_sprite.inl popup-hook condition changed from `tid == 0x02` to `(tid == 0x02 || tid == 0x06)`; src/battle_tts_screenshot.inl screen-close condition changed from `prev.text_id == 0x02` to `(prev.text_id == 0x02 || prev.text_id == 0x06)`. Defensive OR rather than just reverting to 0x06 because the v0.14.77 BAT (Grat + T-Rex) DID produce `[NOEFFECT-WATCH] Scan detected` entries, which means at least one of those casts had text_id=0x02 — different cast contexts (Magic-cast vs Draw-cast, full vs compacted view, different monsters, different battle states) may use different text_ids and we should be robust to all of them. value=0x32 (decimal 50, the spell-name display duration) plus the surrounding context (sub_48E830 fires within milliseconds in NoEffect_RecordSnapshot's window) provides enough specificity that false-positive Scan detection from non-Scan popups is unlikely. BAT plan: scan ANY monster, then scan a SECOND monster in the SAME battle (or scan the same monster twice if multi-target unavailable). Both should produce a `[NOEFFECT-WATCH] Scan detected` log line and proper `[SCAN-CACHE]` capture and proper auto-announce when each Scan UI window opens. Press key 0 in each Scan window; tiered weakness announce from v0.14.78 should fire correctly for both. After Scan UI close, number keys 1/2/3 should revert to default ally HP behavior (proves OnScanPopupDespawn fired). FILES: src/battle_tts_sprite.inl (~25 lines: condition + comment block), src/battle_tts_screenshot.inl (~15 lines: condition + comment block), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. v0.14.78 weakness-tiering logic in scan_tts.cpp is unchanged — it was correct in v0.14.78, but never got exercised on the second scan because that scan was never detected.  // v0.14.78: Status weaknesses tiered — byte <=5 'Highly vulnerable to ...', byte 6..50 'Vulnerable to ...', byte 51..99 silent, byte >=100 announced via FormatStatusResistances on key 9. v0.14.77 BAT confirmed BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE = +0x80 is correct (Grat/T-Rex bytes match canon FF8 design including Sleep=50 'tutorial midpoint'), but the flat byte == 0 weakness threshold missed canonical FF8 vulnerabilities: T-Rex's wiki-documented Sleep / Darkness / Death weaknesses sit at bytes 50 / 2 / 1 respectively, not 0, so v0.14.77 announced only the byte==0 statuses (Petrify / Silence / Berserk for both Grat and T-Rex). Investigation of the FF8 Scan UI rendering chain in disassembly (sub_84F860 -> sub_84F8D0 -> sub_84FD90 -> sub_49F0A0) confirmed the vanilla Scan UI does NOT display status weakness/resistance at all — only stats and elemental affinities. The 20 status bytes exist solely for the inflict probability formula `% chance to inflict before junction = 100 - byte`; there is no game-side threshold to mirror because the game never surfaces this info. We're exposing tactical data vanilla FF8 hides, so the threshold is a deliberate design choice. THIS BUILD: src/scan_tts.cpp::FormatStatusWeaknesses split into two tiers. byte <= 5 -> 'Highly vulnerable' (near-100% inflict before junction — designer filler-low for guaranteed-land ailments). byte 6..50 -> 'Vulnerable' (junction-amplifiable; 100 spells stacked to ST-Atk-J brings StatusAttack to 200, beating StatusDefense 100+50=150 for byte=50 ailments by 50% per swing). Wording uses two sentences when both tiers populated: 'Highly vulnerable to A, B, and C. Vulnerable to D.' JoinNameList helper unchanged. PREDICTED ANNOUNCEMENTS per v0.14.77 BAT bytes: Grat key 0 — 'Highly vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Darkness, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie. Vulnerable to Sleep.' T-Rexaur key 0 — same shape (Petrify byte differs Grat=1 vs T-Rex=0 but both <=5). Key 9 unchanged from v0.14.77. FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp (FormatStatusWeaknesses body + comment block replaced; ~50 lines net), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. Older changelogs archived to DEVNOTES history. v0.14.74 shipped BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE = 0x4C per the deep research's 'best-supported hypothesis' and v0.14.74 BAT immediately invalidated it (Grat showed alternating 169/251 byte pattern across the entire 20-byte run, contradicting the canonical Sleep-vulnerable Grat). v0.14.74.1 added the [SCAN-STRUCT] 121-byte hex dump to find the real offset; v0.14.74.x..v0.14.76 fought through three intervening cross-battle / popup-detection bugs before v0.14.76 unblocked clean two-enemy [SCAN-STRUCT] capture. v0.14.76 BAT delivered the diagnostic data: Grat (Lv15, monsterId 0x1F) and T-Rexaur (Lv21, monsterId 0x43) BOTH show +0x4C..+0x6B as identical garbage 'A9 FB A9 FB ...' (uninitialized memory or shared scratch buffer), but +0x80..+0x93 differs per-monster AND matches FF Wiki / Quistis tutorial canon for every validated status across both samples (Sleep=0x32 (50%) at idx 7 in BOTH — structural smoking gun matching Quistis 'put Grats to sleep' tutorial; Grat Death=0x01 + T-Rex Death=0x00 both highly vulnerable; Grat Berserk=0x00 vulnerable per canon; positive statuses idx 8-19 all show high values (immune) per canonical FF8 enemy resist profile). The deep-research-predicted disasm threshold `cmp al, 0x64` does NOT exist anywhere in `0x00801000.asm` — that model is wrong, but the +0x80 hypothesis is independent and BAT-validated. THIS BUILD: (1) `BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE` already updated to 0x80 in src/battle_tts.h with full BAT-validation comment block. (2) `src/scan_tts.cpp::SpeakField` case 9 re-enabled to call `FormatStatusResistances` (announces ailments with statusRes byte >= 100, the deep research's 'Strong vs' threshold). (3) `src/scan_tts.cpp::SpeakField` case 0 re-enabled to call `FormatStatusWeaknesses` (announces ailments with statusRes byte == 0, the conservative 'fully vulnerable' threshold from FF Wiki StatusDefense formula). Both formatters were already present from v0.14.74 and are unchanged — only the v0.14.74.1 stub-revert that wired them to 'Not implemented yet.' is reverted. (4) `[SCAN-STRUCT]` 121-byte diagnostic dump stripped from CaptureSnapshot (purpose served — actual offset found). DumpRow and LogStructDump helpers also removed since they're now unreferenced. The `[SCAN-CACHE]`, `[SCAN-ELEM]`, and `[SCAN-STAT]` log lines remain in CaptureSnapshot — those are useful for ongoing validation of element + status reads against canon. BAT plan: (a) scan Grat — key 9 should announce 'Resists' with 12 entries (the positive statuses that show high values; we filter to offensive ailments, so this announce should be much shorter — likely 'No status resistances' since Grat's offensive-ailment indices [Death/Poison/Petrify/Darkness/Silence/Berserk/Zombie/Sleep/Slow/Stop/Doom/SlowPetrify/Confuse] are all < 100 in the +0x80 sample). Key 0 should announce 'Weak to Berserk' (Grat's idx 5 = 0x00 at +0x85) and possibly other ailments with byte==0; the [SCAN-STAT] log will reveal exactly which. (b) scan T-Rexaur — key 0 should announce something close to 'Weak to Death' (idx 0 = 0x00 at +0x80). Other T-Rex bytes from the v0.14.76 BAT: +0x80..+0x87 = '00 01 00 02 00 03 01 32' — Death idx 0 = 0, Petrify idx 2 = 0, Silence idx 4 = 0; idx 1 = 1 (Poison), idx 3 = 2 (Darkness), idx 5 = 3 (Berserk) all small but non-zero so they won't trigger the byte==0 weakness announce — calibration data, may want to relax to byte < N for some N in v0.14.78 if the announce is too sparse. FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp (~30 lines: 2 case bodies replaced + LogStructDump call removed + DumpRow + LogStructDump function bodies removed; ~120 lines net deletion), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. The +0x80 confirmation gives us a BAT-validated foundation to build on — if BAT shows announcement weirdness, the [SCAN-STAT] log lines tell us exactly which bytes need different thresholds, and we adjust formula constants in v0.14.78+ without touching the offset. v0.14.55's popup-hook condition `tid == 0x06 && value == 0x32` in HookedPopupSprite (battle_tts_sprite.inl) AND the matching Scan-window-close condition `prev.text_id == 0x06 && prev.value == 50` in PollPopupRecords (battle_tts_screenshot.inl) both used the WRONG text_id constant. Empirical evidence from v0.14.75 BAT log (Grat scan + T-Rexaur scan attempt): every Scan-cast popup arrives with text_id=0x02, NOT 0x06. text_id=0x06 never fires for any popup type — zero matches across the full 4796-line log. The bug was completely silent because PollBattleMagicId's transition-based fallback in battle_tts_ewm.inl caught FIRST scans in a battle (battle_magic_id transitions 0→39) and that produced apparently-working Grat scans with proper [SCAN-CACHE]/[SCAN-STRUCT] dumps. But REPEAT scans in the same battle (battle_magic_id stays at 39 from the first cast onward) had no transition for the polling fallback to detect, AND the popup-hook primary path was dead, so the action-layer ScanTTS detection never fired. Result: the second+ Scan in any battle produced no Scan announcement, no [SCAN-CACHE] capture, and a spurious 'No effect on <target>' from the watchdog 10 seconds later. Aaron experienced this as 'T-Rexaur scan triggered in compacted view despite the config setting being changed' — a misdiagnosis: it was NOT compacted view (the log shows sub_B687C0 fire #1 firing for the T-Rexaur scan, which compacted view skips entirely), it was the broken popup-hook detection. The Config 'Scan: Always' setting is working correctly; this is a separate code bug introduced in v0.14.55 and undetected for ~21 builds because the magic_id polling fallback masked it for first-of-battle scans (the only kind we'd been informally testing). FIX: change `0x06` → `0x02` in both locations (one each in sprite.inl and screenshot.inl). With the correct text_id, the popup hook reliably catches every Scan cast (first or repeat) via its existing s_lastScanCastTick mechanism; NoEffect_RecordSnapshot consumes the tick within the same frame and calls ScanTTS::OnScanCast directly with the correct slot. PollBattleMagicId's Scan branch becomes a redundant fallback that may produce duplicate [SCAN-CACHE]/[SCAN-STRUCT] log lines on first-of-battle scans; harmless cosmetic noise that we can clean up later if it bothers anyone (OnScanCast's per-slot 30 s lock from v0.14.57 prevents duplicate user-facing announcements). Side benefit: the matching screen-close detection fix (`prev.text_id == 0x02 && prev.value == 50` in screenshot.inl::PollPopupRecords) means OnScanPopupDespawn now actually fires when the Scan UI closes, properly clearing s_scanScreenActiveSlot so number keys 1..0 revert to default ally-HP behavior. That was a latent bug never reported because Aaron hadn't yet pressed number keys after closing the Scan UI in any past BAT. THIS FIX UNBLOCKS THE PRIMARY NEXT-SESSION PRIORITY (BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE three-enemy diagnostic per NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT). With repeat scans now working, Aaron can scan Grat → T-Rexaur → Tonberry in any sequence and we'll get clean [SCAN-STRUCT] dumps for all three to diff-find the correct status resist offset. FILES: src/battle_tts_sprite.inl (~5 lines: condition flipped + comment block extended explaining the v0.14.55 transcription error), src/battle_tts_screenshot.inl (~5 lines: condition flipped + comment block extended), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new behavior beyond the fix; no version bump in scan_tts.cpp; no schema changes; no new addresses. BAT plan: enter Balamb Garden Training Center, force a multi-Grat encounter or a Grat→T-Rexaur sequence, cast Scan twice (or scan two different enemies in the same battle), confirm both produce proper [SCAN-CACHE]+[SCAN-STRUCT] log output AND a proper auto-announce when each Scan UI window opens AND no spurious [SPELL-NOEFFECT] / [NOEFFECT-FLUSH] entries. After BAT pass, this directly unblocks v0.14.74.1's [SCAN-STRUCT] diagnostic which now has reliable repeat-scan capture, so the BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE three-enemy diff can proceed in v0.14.77+. The v0.14.74.1 stub-revert of keys 9/0 (FormatStatusResistances/FormatStatusWeaknesses unwired, 'Not implemented yet.') is unchanged in this build and ships as-is."

// v0.14.75: Permanent screenshot trigger relocated from F12 to F11 + dead F11 diagnostics removed + AnnounceMenuSummary moved to M.

// v0.14.74.4-diag: F12 = on-demand screenshot capture (diagnostic build). Aaron needs to verify whether FF8's in-game Config menu has a 'Scan compacted-view' toggle before we invest in compacted-view fallback code for the Scan UI auto-announce architecture. The mod can already capture FF8's OpenGL framebuffer (via the SwapBuffers hook + glReadPixels installed in BattleTTS::Initialize), but only at hard-coded trigger points (kind4 sprite spawns, scan UI fire #1, etc.). Standard Windows screenshot tools (PrintScreen, snipping tool, GDI BitBlt) return solid black for FF8 because the OpenGL framebuffer isn't in the Windows compositor pipeline, so Aaron can't capture the Config menu manually. ARCHITECTURE: in src/dinput8.cpp's AccessibilityThread main hotkey block — alongside the existing F1/F2/F3-F8/V handlers — added an F12 edge-detector. On press, builds an absolute path 'Logs/screenshots/f12_<HHMMSS>_<MS>' (no extension) under BattleTTS::GetScreenshotDir(), calls BattleTTS::RequestScreenshotAsync(path) with default 0 frame delay, logs '[F12-SCREENSHOT] Capture requested: <path>.png' to ff8_mod.log, and speaks 'Screenshot captured.' via ScreenReader::Speak so Aaron knows the keypress registered. RequestScreenshotAsync sets a flag on the global SwapBuffers hook; the next swap (any game state — menus, field, battle, all share the same hook) writes <path>.bmp + <path>.png to disk. F12 RULE COMPLIANCE per userMemories: searched all source files for VK_F12, F12, and 0x7B keycode references before adding the new handler. Found one orphaned ScanTTS::PollDiagnosticKey forward decl in src/battle_tts.cpp's ScanTTS namespace block (left over from v0.14.66-diag's scan-type-label dump that was already removed in some earlier cleanup but never fully cleaned) — REMOVED. The 'F12 reserved' comment in src/field_nav_handlekeys.inl is just a comment, no code, left as-is. The 'F12 is handled by FieldNavigation::HandleKeys()' comment in dinput8.cpp updated to reflect the new ownership. Confirmed scan_tts.cpp / scan_tts.h / battle_tts_hp.inl have no PollDiagnosticKey or F12 keycode reference. F12 has exactly one consumer now — the screenshot trigger in dinput8.cpp. CONTEXT: blocked on the v0.14.74.3 BAT result — Aaron tested twice. Test 1 (success, world map outside Garden): Bite Bug -> escape -> Fastitocalon. v0.14.74.3 fingerprint fix worked perfectly, Fastitocalon properly identified. Test 2 (compacted-view issue, Balamb Garden Training Center): Grat -> escape -> T-Rexaur. T-Rexaur correctly identified (fingerprint fix worked!) but Scan only spoke the description without prompting for number keys and without waiting for confirm — auto-advanced. HYPOTHESIS: T-Rexaur Scan rendered in FF8's compacted view (shown when scanning a monster ID that's already been scanned in the current save/session). Action-layer (sub_48D200 popup hook) fires for both compacted and full views, captures snapshot to s_scanCache[slot]. But sub_B687C0 only fires for the FULL Scan UI render — compacted view skips it. The auto-announce trigger (s_scanScreenActiveSlot activation) is gated on sub_B687C0 first-fire (v0.14.60 architecture). So in compacted view: data captured silently, no auto-announce, number keys don't activate. v0.14.54 BAT proved both sub_B687C0 and sub_84F860 skip compacted view; v0.14.55 moved to action-layer, then v0.14.59+ UX redesign re-coupled the user-facing announce to sub_B687C0. NEXT STEP after this BAT: if Config menu has no Scan toggle (highly likely — vanilla FF8 PC config menu is just Cursor / ATB / Battle Speed / Battle Message Speed / Sound / Music / Magic Order / Window Color), v0.14.74.5+ adds compacted-view fallback in ScanTTS::OnScanCast(_, true) — ~800 ms timeout-based fallback: if sub_B687C0 doesn't fire within the window, treat as compacted view, speak the auto-announce now, set s_scanScreenActiveSlot for ~15-20 s so number keys work without the despawn signal. FILES: src/dinput8.cpp (~30 lines added in main hotkey block), src/battle_tts.cpp (~5 lines removed: orphaned PollDiagnosticKey forward decl + comment), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO behavior change for normal Aaron play except the new F12 hotkey.

// v0.14.74.3: Cross-battle stale ENEMY NAME bug fix — second stale-data vector exposed by v0.14.74.2 BAT. ROOT CAUSE: in src/battle_tts.cpp::AnnounceBattleStart, the readiness gate is just 'allyMaxHP > 0 AND BATTLE_INIT_MIN_DELAY_MS elapsed'. When the player escapes battle 1 and immediately enters battle 2 with a different formation, the engine does NOT zero enemy slot memory on exit and does NOT immediately repopulate it on the next entry — it leaves the previous battle's enemy data (HP=587/MaxHP=587/Lv=21 status=0x00 for Grats in the BAT log) in place for ~8-10 seconds before writing the new formation. Squall's allyMaxHP=901 survives the transition, so the existing readiness gate fires immediately at the 2-second min delay; CountActiveEnemies() returns true (stale Grat HP > 0); BuildEnemyNameString reads stale memory and announces the previous battle's names. Worse, s_enemyAnnounceDone is set true, disabling the second-pass safety net that would have caught the late population. Aaron's BAT 19:47:48: entered T-Rexaur battle, mod announced 'Battle! 2 Grats.' against the audible T-Rexaur roar; engine wrote real T-Rexaur HP at 19:47:58 (+10 s) but cache was already locked. FIX: snapshot enemy slot fingerprint (HP/MaxHP/Lv/status for slots 3-6) at OnBattleExit; in AnnounceBattleStart and the Update() second-pass loop, if enemyCount > 0 but the live fingerprint matches the snapshot bit-for-bit AND we're inside BATTLE_INIT_TIMEOUT_MS, treat enemies as 'not ready' and defer to second-pass. New helper EnemySlotsMatchLastBattleSnap() does the comparison under SEH guard. New struct EnemySlotSnap; new state s_lastBattleEnemySnap[BATTLE_TOTAL_SLOTS - BATTLE_ALLY_SLOTS] + s_lastBattleEnemySnapValid. BATTLE_INIT_TIMEOUT_MS bumped from 10000 to 15000 ms because the engine took ~10 s to repopulate T-Rexaur data in the BAT (right at the old timeout boundary). FIRST-BATTLE-OF-SESSION SAFE: snapValid=false makes the helper return false, preserving existing behavior. VICTORY EXIT SAFE: ending HP=0/status=0x01 differs from a fresh battle's HP>0/status=0x00 — fingerprints differ — no false delay. ESCAPE EXIT (THE BUG CASE): slot data persists in memory, fresh battle reuses same memory, fingerprints match, defer to second-pass which retries every frame until engine writes new data. SAME-FORMATION RE-ENCOUNTER: fingerprints match for the full 15-second timeout, then we fall through and announce — worst case is a 15 s announce delay on legitimate same-formation re-encounters, an acceptable cost for not lying about what enemies are present. NEW DIAGNOSTIC LOGS: [EXIT-SNAP] in OnBattleExit dumps the captured fingerprint per battle exit; [STALE-ENEMY] in AnnounceBattleStart fires whenever the stale-fingerprint path defers the first-pass announce. FILES: src/battle_tts.cpp (~5 edits totaling ~140 lines, all comment-heavy), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The v0.14.74.2 magicId fix is preserved exactly; this build runs alongside it. Test plan: cast Scan on enemies in battle 1, escape, walk into battle 2 with a DIFFERENT formation. Expected: 'Battle!' generic announce at the 2-second mark, then the correct enemy name announced ~8-10 s later via second-pass when engine populates fresh data. Pre-fix observed (v0.14.74.2 BAT): announces previous battle's enemy names in new battle. NoEffect-on-Scan suppression failure (Aaron heard 'No effect on Grat 1' for a T-Rexaur scan) is a downstream symptom of this same root cause and should also be fixed by this build, since the slot-3 entity wasn't actually a Grat — the action-layer Scan detection was operating on whatever slot data the engine had at the time of the cast (real T-Rexaur), but the cached enemy name was Grat from stale memory. Once enemy data is properly fresh by the time Scan fires, the detection path's name resolution will be correct and the watchdog cancel can fire on the right (slot, name) pair. v0.14.74.1 [SCAN-STRUCT] diagnostic still pending BAT — unchanged in this build.

// v0.14.74.2: Critical correctness fix for stale Scan / GF announces leaking across battle escape. PRIMARY FIX in src/battle_tts.cpp::OnBattleEnter: replaced 's_prevBattleMagicId = -1' with a SEH-guarded read of *s_battleMagicIdAddr to cache the engine's current battle_magic_id value at battle entry. ROOT CAUSE: Setting prev to -1 made every battle's first PollBattleMagicId call see the residual value left over from the previous battle (39 = Scan after a Scan cast, 200 = Ifrit after a summon, etc.) as a fresh -1 -> stale_value transition, which fired ScanTTS::OnScanCast or GfAudioDesc::OnGFAnimationStart against whatever target bitmask was also still in memory at 0x01D76884. Most visible failure mode: escape after casting Scan, enter next battle, hear a stale Scan announce against whatever slot the previous battle's bitmask resolved to (or a no-op SCAN-TTS log line if the bitmask resolved to invalid). Same class of bug applied to GF effect IDs: escape after summoning Ifrit, enter next battle, hear Ifrit's audio description replayed at battle entry. The fix caches the live value so PollBattleMagicId sees prev == cur and detects no transition; once the engine eventually clears battle_magic_id to a fresh value (typically 0), that transition is non-actionable (neither GF effect ID nor 39). v0.14.74.2 BAT result: PRIMARY FIX VERIFIED — [SCAN-TTS] Battle entry: cached current magicId=39 as prev fired correctly on the post-escape battle 2 entry, suppressing the stale Scan that would otherwise have fired. But BAT exposed a SECOND stale-data vector (enemy name cache reading stale slot memory) addressed in v0.14.74.3 above. DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH FIX in src/battle_tts_sprite.inl::ResetSpriteSpawnState: added InterlockedExchange clearing s_lastScanCastTick to 0 per battle. Without this, escaping within SCAN_CAST_RECENT_MS (1 sec) of casting Scan could leak the popup-hook tick across the battle boundary and trigger a bogus action-layer Scan announce on the very first sub_48E830 fire of the next battle (NoEffect_RecordSnapshot's InterlockedExchange normally consumes the tick within the same frame the popup hook sets it, but the fix is one line and the race window is real). FILES: src/battle_tts.cpp (~30 lines added at the s_prevBattleMagicId reset site, comment-heavy because the bug is subtle), src/battle_tts_sprite.inl (1 line of code + ~10 lines of comment in ResetSpriteSpawnState), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

// v0.14.74.1: Diagnostic build for the broken keys 9/0 from v0.14.74. Two changes scoped to src/scan_tts.cpp. (1) NEW [SCAN-STRUCT] diagnostic log in CaptureSnapshot dumps 121 bytes of the entity struct from +0x3C through +0xB4 every scan, split across 8 log lines for greppability. Range covers the elemental block anchor (+0x3C..+0x4B, 16 bytes — should match [SCAN-ELEM] u16 values, validates dump alignment), the unknown gap (+0x4C..+0xB3, 104 bytes — contains the 20-byte status resistance block somewhere), and the level anchor (+0xB4, 1 byte — should equal snap.level, confirms alignment held all the way down). New helpers DumpRow (formats one 16-byte hex row) and LogStructDump (calls DumpRow 8 times under SEH guard). v0.14.74 BAT decisively proved BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE = 0x4C is wrong: Grat scanned with alternating 169/251 byte pattern across all 20 indices, contradicting canon (Grat is the in-game source of Sleep magic but the v0.14.74 announcement said Grat 'Resists Sleep'). The deep research itself flagged that offset as a 'best-supported hypothesis' and explicitly recommended runtime validation; this dump provides the validation. Plan A diagnostic-driven path chosen over Plan B disassembly hunt because Plan B's specific formulation (cmp al, 0x64 in sub_84F860 phase handlers) didn't pan out — those phase handlers don't read entity offsets in the suspect range at all; the threshold check lives in the status APPLICATION path, not the scan UI rendering path, so finding it via disasm would require a much wider hunt. (2) Keys 9 / 0 in SpeakField switch reverted to 'Not implemented yet.' so misleading status info doesn't ship to Aaron during play — FormatStatusResistances and FormatStatusWeaknesses still exist and are unchanged but no longer wired. Keys 1-8 stay exactly as v0.14.74 (offensive/defensive stats, elemental resistances + weaknesses all working). Test plan for next BAT: scan three enemies with known-different status profiles and diff the [SCAN-STRUCT] sections in ff8_battle.log. Grat (vulnerable to Sleep/Silence/Berserk per Quistis Training Center tutorial canon) should show 0x00 at the indices for those statuses. T-Rexaur (vulnerable to Sleep/Darkness/Death/Poison per FF8 wiki) should show 0x00 at those indices and high values at others. Tonberry (no documented status weaknesses) should show high values across the entire 20-byte block. The 20-byte run where these patterns line up consistently is the actual BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE; v0.14.74.2 will update battle_tts.h with the corrected offset and re-enable keys 9/0. Files: src/scan_tts.cpp (DumpRow + LogStructDump helpers, LogStructDump call in CaptureSnapshot, SpeakField cases 9/0 reverted to 'Not implemented yet.' stubs), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). All other v0.14.74 mechanisms (split stats, combined elemental resistances on key 7, FormatWeak on key 8) preserved exactly.

// v0.14.74: Scan UI keyboard layout overhaul. Splits the 8-stat block into offensive (key 5: STR/MAG/SPD/HIT) and defensive (key 6: VIT/SPR/EVA/LCK) readouts — hearing all 8 numbers in one announcement was overwhelming. Consolidates v0.14.73.1's separate Absorbs (key 7) and Nullifies (key 8) into a single combined Resistances readout on key 7 that announces Halves / Strongly resists / Nullifies / Absorbs as four sentence-separated buckets (e.g. 'Halves Fire and Earth. Strongly resists Ice. Nullifies Thunder. Absorbs Water and Holy.'). The 901..999 'Strongly resists' range — left ambiguous and silent in v0.14.73.1 — is now folded into key 7 alongside the other resistance categories. Elemental Weaknesses (FormatWeak) moves from key 6 to key 8 (logic unchanged, threshold still v < 800). NEW key 9: Status Resistances — reads 20 u8 bytes from entity+0x4C (BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE) and announces ailments where statusRes byte >= 100 (the deep research's 'Strong vs' threshold). NEW key 0: Status Weaknesses — announces ailments where statusRes byte == 0 (the conservative 'fully vulnerable' threshold inferred from the FF Wiki StatusDefense formula; T-Rexaur's BAT is the canonical anchor for calibration). Both keys 9 and 0 filter to 13 offensive ailments only (Death/Poison/Petrify/Darkness/Silence/Berserk/Zombie/Sleep/Slow/Stop/Doom/Slow Petrify/Confuse) — buffs (Haste/Regen/Reflect/Float) and non-offensive effects (Drain/Expulsion/???) excluded since they're not actionable for the player. The active-statuses readout that used to live on key 0 in v0.14.73 is removed because the same info is announced via the target-cursor flow in BuildStatusString already — net win for the layout (10 useful keys instead of 9 useful + 1 redundant). NEW HEADER CONSTANT: BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE = 0x4C in src/battle_tts.h with full encoding documentation. NEW HELPER: ReadSlotStatusRes() in src/scan_tts.cpp mirrors ReadSlotElements (SEH-guarded memcpy of 20 bytes from entity_base + BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE). NEW LOG LINE: [SCAN-STAT] in CaptureSnapshot dumps all 20 raw status bytes per scan in deep-research order (Death/Poison/Petrify/Darkness/Silence/Berserk/Zombie/Sleep/Haste/Slow/Stop/Regen/Reflect/Doom/SlowPetrify/Float/Confuse/Drain/Expulsion/Unknown) so the byte == 0 weakness threshold can be data-driven if T-Rexaur's BAT shows different values. NEW FORMATTERS: FormatOffensiveStats + FormatDefensiveStats (replace FormatStats), FormatResistances (replaces FormatAbsorb + FormatNullify; folds in Halves and Strongly resists), FormatStatusResistances (key 9), FormatStatusWeaknesses (key 0). REFACTORED HELPER: JoinElementList -> JoinNameList parameterized over a name table (now used by both element formatters via ELEMENT_NAMES and status formatters via STATUS_NAMES_20). NEW DATA: STATUS_NAMES_20 (all 20 status names in deep-research order, used by [SCAN-STAT] log + status formatters), OFFENSIVE_AILMENT_INDICES (13-element filter table), OFFENSIVE_AILMENT_COUNT. SpeakField switch updated for new bindings. BuildAutoAnnounce comment block updated to reference the v0.14.74 key map (spoken phrase unchanged — still 'Press numbers 0 through 9 for details.'). Files: src/battle_tts.h (BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE constant), src/scan_tts.cpp (~280 lines: ReadSlotStatusRes, [SCAN-STAT] log, FormatOffensiveStats/FormatDefensiveStats, JoinNameList refactor, FormatResistances, FormatStatusResistances, FormatStatusWeaknesses, STATUS_NAMES_20, OFFENSIVE_AILMENT_INDICES, SpeakField switch, comment blocks), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). Expected v0.14.74 BAT outcomes: (1) Fastitocalon should announce on key 7: 'Halves Fire. Absorbs Water.' (820 in halves bucket, 1000 in absorb bucket); on key 8: 'Weak against Thunder and Earth.' (700 and 650). (2) T-Rexaur should announce on key 0: something like 'Weak to Death, Poison, Darkness, and Sleep.' if standard FF8 data has those bytes at 0 — this confirms or calibrates the threshold; if T-Rexaur's Sleep byte is non-zero but small (50, 30, etc.), the [SCAN-STAT] log reveals it and we adjust to byte < N. (3) Tonberry (no documented status weaknesses) should announce 'No status weaknesses.' as a null-test. (4) The 8-stat readout no longer happens on key 5 — instead key 5 says 'Strength X. Magic X. Speed X. Hit X.' and key 6 says 'Vitality X. Spirit X. Evasion X. Luck X.' — each ~half the cognitive load. (5) Active-statuses readout removed from key 0 — if Aaron misses it later, status info still arrives via target-cursor announcements during normal battle navigation."

// v0.14.72: Resolve sub_47EC70 hook conflict between SCAN-TTS and victory TTS by removing the standalone scan_tts.cpp hook installer (v0.14.68-diag through v0.14.71) and routing through the victory module's existing canonical hook (HookedBtCandidate1 in battle_tts_victory.inl, installed since v0.13.14 via InstallBattleTextHooks). v0.14.71 BAT log decisively exposed the conflict at module init: '[BT-HOOK] sub_47EC70 @ 0x0047EC70: E9 EB F4 A0 71 8B 04 45' (the E9 JMP rel32 opcode confirmed scan_tts.cpp's hook installed first) followed by '[BT-HOOK] sub_47EC70 MH_CreateHook FAILED: 3' (MH_ERROR_ALREADY_CREATED for the victory installer). MinHook silently lost the race for whichever installer ran second, so victory phase detection (EXP/Items/GF/Ability announcements via text_id 22/23/21/28/109/121/127 routing in HookedBtCandidate1) has been broken since v0.14.68-diag. Aaron confirmed in this session that he had noticed victory TTS not announcing post-Scan but hadn't reported it because we were focused on Scan itself. ARCHITECTURE FIX: scan_tts.cpp's HookedGetBattleText converted to public ScanTTS::HandleBattleText(int textId, const char* result); the BATTLE_GET_TEXT_ADDR / GetBattleTextFn typedef / s_originalGetBattleText / s_getBattleTextHookInstalled / InstallGetBattleTextHook all removed; the InstallGetBattleTextHook call in Initialize replaced with a no-op comment explaining the new ownership. battle_tts_victory.inl's HookedBtCandidate1 gains a one-line forward call '::ScanTTS::HandleBattleText((int)a1, (const char*)result)' immediately after s_origBt1 returns, before the victory-mode-only logic block (mode==4/5/100). HandleBattleText is internally gated on GetScanFlightSlot() so it's a near-no-op outside an active scan event — critical because the victory hook fires on every sub_47EC70 invocation (~hundreds per battle frame). All v0.14.71 SCAN-TTS behavior preserved: Fly-types still capture 'Fly Monster' on key 3, type-less monsters still correctly fall back to plain 'Level N.', wider gate via GetScanFlightSlot still active, per-scan reset in OnScanCast still defensive. Forward declaration of HandleBattleText added to scan_tts.h public API and to the existing ScanTTS namespace forward-decl block in battle_tts.cpp (matches the existing v0.14.66-diag pattern for PollDiagnosticKey). Files: src/scan_tts.h (public decl), src/scan_tts.cpp (~30 lines removed: hook machinery deleted, Initialize updated), src/battle_tts.cpp (1 forward decl added to ScanTTS namespace block), src/battle_tts_victory.inl (1 forward call added in HookedBtCandidate1), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). v0.14.72 BAT result: PASS. First 8 bytes of sub_47EC70 now read '8B 44 24 04 66 8B 04 45' (the original prologue, NOT E9 ...); '[BT-HOOK] sub_47EC70 hooked OK'; '[BT-HOOK] 8/8 hooks installed' (was 7/8 since v0.14.68-diag). Scan TTS still works perfectly; victory TTS phase announcements function again post-Scan for the first time in 4 sessions.

// v0.14.69: PRODUCTION build wiring the Scan UI's monster-type label into the Level announcement (key 3). v0.14.68-diag's hook on sub_47EC70 (get_battle_text) BAT decisively cracked the type-label architecture: the on-screen 'Fly Monster' / 'Earth Monster' / etc. label at the bottom-left is rendered by TWO consecutive sub_47EC70 calls during a Scan UI session — first the type prefix (text_id varies per monster type, e.g. text_id=99 returns 'Fly' for Glacial Eye), then text_id=36 which is the universal 'Monster' suffix. Decoded via FF8 text encoding (uppercase encoded = decoded + 4, lowercase encoded = decoded - 2): text_id=99 hex=4A 6A 77 00 → 'Fly', text_id=36 hex=51 6D 6C 71 72 63 70 00 → 'Monster'. Visually confirmed in scan_001352_348_slot3_Glacial_Eye.png — bottom-left renders 'Fly Monster' exactly. v0.14.69 BAT result: Bite Bug worked perfectly ('Level 8, Fly Monster.'); Fastitocalon failed to capture (Level key 3 said just 'Level 8.'); Caterchipillar wasn't actually scanned (Aaron encountered the battle but didn't cast Scan).  // v0.14.66-diag: F12-gated diagnostic to hunt down the memory location of the 'Fly Monster' / 'Earth Monster' / etc. monster-type label rendered at the bottom-left of the Scan UI. The Bite Bug screenshot from v0.14.65.3 BAT exposed this previously-unseen UI element — we don't yet announce it. FFNx canary's ff8.h documents only scan_text_positions / scan_text_data for the Scan UI (no separate type table named), and the strings 'Fly Monster' / 'Fly' / 'Monster' are NOT in FF8_EN.exe as plain ASCII (FF8_EN_strings.txt confirms), so the type text lives in some encoded data table we haven't located. APPROACH: when Aaron presses F12 while a Scan window is active, ScanTTS::PollDiagnosticKey() (new) dumps to ff8_battle.log under the [SCAN-TYPE-DIAG] tag: (1) the full 0xD0-byte entity struct for the active scan slot — the type might be a single byte stored among the unknown-meaning offsets, (2) 0x80 bytes BEFORE scan_text_positions (0x01887474) looking for a parallel positions table, (3) the full scan_text_positions table (0x140 bytes for ~160 monster_id entries) for cross-reference, (4) the first 0x100 bytes of scan_text_data (0x018875B4), (5) 16-bit reads at id*2 from 9 candidate base addresses around scan_text_positions ± 0x200, (6) 8-bit reads at id from 6 candidate base addresses around scan_text_data, all SEH-guarded and ASCII-gutter formatted. Aaron presses F12 for several enemies of varied types (Bite Bug = Fly Monster, plus Geezard or Caterchipillar of a different type) and we diff the dumps to find which bytes encode the type. Wired in via a new public ScanTTS::PollDiagnosticKey() (forward decl in scan_tts.h, impl at end of scan_tts.cpp before the namespace closer) called from PollHPCheckKeys() in battle_tts_hp.inl on every battle-frame poll — internally edge-detects F12 (s_diagF12WasDown) and gates on IsScreenActive() so it's a cheap no-op outside its narrow active window. F12 was already verified free across all source files (no existing VK_F12 / 0x7B handlers). DELIVERABLE: this build produces no behavior change for Aaron's normal play — just adds the F12 hotkey for the diagnostic dump. Once Aaron BATs with two or three enemies of varied types and uploads ff8_battle.log, we'll diff the [SCAN-TYPE-DIAG] sections and identify the type field. Files: scan_tts.h (forward decl), scan_tts.cpp (impl + DumpHexWindow helper, ~190 lines added at end of file), battle_tts.cpp (forward decl in ScanTTS namespace block), battle_tts_hp.inl (one-line call site at top of PollHPCheckKeys), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The v0.14.65.3 frame-delay screenshot mechanism is unchanged — still in production for the auto-capture during scan UI render. v0.14.65.2 BAT confirmed the path fix worked perfectly (PNG landed at 'Logs\\screenshots\\scan_234209_596_slot3_Fastitocalon.png' which Claude can read directly without manual copying), but the captured image showed the same too-early render as v0.14.65.1: stat labels visible (HP / STR DEF / INT SPI / DEX EVA) with NO numeric values rendered, plus typewriter description showing only 'Fast' and 'A fi' partial chars. Stats announce was perfect ('Strength 20. Vitality 132. Magic 56. Spirit 180. Speed 5. Luck 0. Evasion 6. Hit 0.' for Lv14 Fastitocalon). The capture was happening on the very next swap after fire #1, before FF8's typewriter could render values. FIX: extend the existing async screenshot machinery with a frame-delay counter. New static volatile int s_captureFrameDelay alongside s_captureRequested in battle_tts_screenshot.inl. HookedSwapBuffers decrements the counter each frame and only calls DoGLCapture() when it reaches 0. RequestScreenshotAsync now takes an optional frameDelay parameter (default 0 preserves v0.14.65 behavior; existing callers including the synchronous CaptureScreenshot path are unaffected). scan_tts.cpp passes 90 (≈1.5 s at 60 fps) which gives the engine ample time to draw the full description (~95 chars) plus all 6 numeric stat values. Description renders ~1.5 chars per frame = ~63 frames; stat values typically render in another ~10-15 frames, so 90 frames has comfortable margin. User won't notice the visual delay since Aaron is blind — only the diagnostic capture is being deferred. The scan UI stays open for ~6 s before any keypress (BAT log shows window opened at 23:42:09 and key 5 pressed at 23:42:15) so the delayed capture can't accidentally catch a different UI state. CaptureScreenshot (synchronous) explicitly resets s_captureFrameDelay to 0 so its 160 ms Sleep-and-poll contract is preserved — only the async path uses the delay. Files: battle_tts_screenshot.inl (s_captureFrameDelay declaration + HookedSwapBuffers counter check + CaptureScreenshot reset), battle_tts.h (RequestScreenshotAsync gets default frameDelay=0 parameter), battle_tts.cpp (RequestScreenshotAsync impl reads frameDelay and clamps negatives to 0), scan_tts.cpp (call site passes 90 + log message updated to mention the delay), ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

// v0.14.65.2: Fix the screenshot path so scan_*.png captures land in the project's Logs\\screenshots directory alongside kind4_*, poll_NEW_*, popup_time_* etc., instead of FF8.exe's CWD (Steam install dir's Screenshots folder, where Claude has no read access). BAT result: PASS. Path now lands at 'C:\\Users\\ampag\\OneDrive\\Documents\\FFVIII-Accessibility-Mod\\FF8_OriginalPC_mod\\Logs\\screenshots\\scan_234209_596_slot3_Fastitocalon.{bmp,png}'. Image content matches v0.14.65.1 (same too-early render pattern — labels visible, no values yet, typewriter partial), confirming the path fix didn't change capture timing. Same too-early issue addressed in v0.14.65.3 with the frame-delay counter. FIX: added BattleTTS::GetScreenshotDir() public accessor that returns KIND4_SCREENSHOT_DIR (file-static const char* in battle_tts_sprite.inl, only visible inside battle_tts.cpp's translation unit). scan_tts.cpp HookedScanGetText now composes the absolute path from this accessor instead of using a relative 'Screenshots\\' prefix.

// v0.14.65.1: Fix the auto-screenshot trigger that didn't fire in v0.14.65 BAT. ROOT CAUSE: I assumed sub_B687C0 fires per-frame while the scan UI is open. v0.14.65 BAT log proved otherwise — between fire #1 (window-open) and the user closing the scan window 38 seconds later, sub_B687C0 fired exactly ONCE. So my count==30 trigger was unreachable. FIX: move the screenshot trigger from the count==30 branch to inside the count==1 branch, right after OnScanPopupSpawn(). v0.14.65 stats wiring (Part A) is unchanged — BAT confirmed perfect: announced 'Strength 16. Vitality 129. Magic 50. Spirit 180. Speed 5. Luck 0. Evasion 6. Hit 0.' for Fastitocalon Lv10, matching [SCAN-CACHE] log values exactly.

// v0.14.65: Two complementary changes — Stats key 5 wired (BAT'd PASS) + auto-screenshot capture (BAT'd FAIL — trigger never fired, fixed in v0.14.65.1).

// v0.14.64: Strip SC1-PROBE and SC2-PROBE diagnostics. v0.14.63 BAT confirmed everything works correctly: 'Cast Fire'-type spell-cast banners announce again, scan announce works cleanly with no duplication, and the IsScreenActive()-based gate correctly suppresses only the rendered scan-window text. The probes were added in v0.14.60 (SC2-PROBE) and v0.14.61 (SC1-PROBE) to identify the source of the duplicate scan announcement; that work is complete. Probes were just adding log noise on every speech call, so this build removes them along with the now-unused <intrin.h> include for _ReturnAddress(). Single-channel SAPI mode from v0.14.61 STAYS in place (s_pVoice2 = nullptr in InitSAPI) — Aaron prefers it and it eliminates the simultaneous-overlap class of bugs entirely. The v0.14.60 sub_B687C0 first-fire announce trigger STAYS — it's the correct architecture. The v0.14.62/63 Hook_show_dialog gate (suppress only when ScanTTS::IsScreenActive() during battle) STAYS — it correctly suppresses scan duplicates while letting all other battle UI text through. Files: screen_reader.cpp (3 deletions — SC1-PROBE block in Speak, SC2-PROBE block in SpeakChannel2, intrin.h include), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The Scan TTS chapter (v0.14.50-64) is now closed; next priority returns to v0.14.65+ implementing remaining Scan field queries: stats (key 5), elemental affinity (keys 6/7/8), status resist (key 9), active statuses (key 0).

// v0.14.63: Narrow Hook_show_dialog battle-mode suppression to scan-active period only. v0.14.62 BAT confirmed Scan announce works perfectly with no duplications, but Aaron noticed an unintended regression: the 'Cast Fire'-type spell-cast banner that appears at the top of the screen when a character begins casting a spell stopped announcing. That banner rides on Hook_show_dialog (universal text renderer), and v0.14.62's blanket 'mode == 3 → suppress' gate silenced it along with the scan duplicate. Fix: narrow the suppression gate from 'currentMode == 3' to 'currentMode == 3 && ScanTTS::IsScreenActive()'. ScanTTS::IsScreenActive() returns true only between OnScanPopupSpawn (first sub_B687C0 fire — when the scan window actually renders) and OnScanPopupDespawn (when the player dismisses it). During that period — and only that period — the rendered scan text would duplicate the scan_tts.cpp auto-announce, so we suppress. Outside that period (including all other battle moments), 'Cast Fire' / 'Cast Cure' / 'Cast Scan' / mid-battle cutscene dialog / any other battle window text speaks normally through show_dialog. Sequencing safety: HookedScanGetText sets s_scanScreenActiveSlot via InterlockedExchange BEFORE returning the text to the engine, and Hook_show_dialog reads window text AFTER calling the original (which is what triggers sub_B687C0 internally). So by the time we check IsScreenActive() in show_dialog's speak path, the flag is already set on the first scan render — the very first show_dialog call for the scan window correctly suppresses. Files: field_dialog.cpp (added ScanTTS forward decl + replaced v0.14.62 gate with IsScreenActive() gate), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The v0.10.112 'Received <item>' draw-result rewrite ('Squall received 4 Blizzards') still applies and is now permitted always in battle since it never coincides with scan being active. Note that the v0.14.62 'isDrawReceived' bypass flag is no longer needed and was removed; the rewrite logic above the gate stays in place. SC1-PROBE and SC2-PROBE diagnostic instrumentation kept active for v0.14.63 BAT verification; will strip in v0.14.64 once Aaron confirms both scan AND 'Cast Fire' work cleanly. Single-channel SAPI mode also stays.

// v0.14.62: Gate Hook_show_dialog speak path to non-battle modes only. v0.14.61 BAT (single-channel SAPI + SC1-PROBE) proved Aaron heard the duplicate twice but serialized instead of simultaneous — single-channel worked as expected. The SC1-PROBE caught THREE Voice 1 calls at the exact moment of the Scan announce: caller=0x6DC65684 (the char* Speak wrapper), all queue-mode (interrupt=0), with content matching the three text sections of the FF8 Scan UI window: 'A fish that swims in the ground...' (description), 'Fastitocalon' (name), 'LEVEL 14 HP ?????/?????' (stats line with hidden-HP markers). Source identified: Hook_show_dialog in src/field_dialog.cpp. The hook intercepts the universal text renderer at FF8Addresses::show_dialog_addr (per the v04.17 comment, hooked for MODE_TUTO coverage) and decodes any new window text into ScreenReader::Speak via the queue-mode path. ScanTTS already owns scan-window announces via the v0.14.60 architecture (sub_B687C0 first-fire — the same engine call that triggers the rendered text fetch); this hook re-speaks those same text fetches as separate utterances. Fix: in the speak path of Hook_show_dialog, when currentMode == 3 (battle), suppress the speak unless decoded text matches the v0.10.112 'Received <item>' draw-result path (which is still valid because that's a battle-specific feature owned by field_dialog, not ScanTTS). All other battle window text is silently dropped (still logged as [SHOW_DIALOG-SUPPRESS] for diagnostic auditing). Trade-off: any other battle UI text that PREVIOUSLY relied on this hook (e.g. battle dialog windows from cutscenes that play during fights) will go silent until we explicitly route them through BattleTTS. Acceptable risk — we'll catch missing announces during regression and add explicit handling. Files: field_dialog.cpp (gate), ff8_accessibility.h (version). SC1-PROBE and SC2-PROBE diagnostic instrumentation kept active for v0.14.62 BAT verification; will strip in v0.14.63 once the duplicate is confirmed gone. v0.14.61 single-channel mode also kept — separate concern from the duplicate source, but Aaron has confirmed he prefers single-channel regardless.

// v0.14.61: Single-channel SAPI mode + SC1-PROBE diagnostic. v0.14.60 BAT showed Aaron still heard simultaneous duplicate speech during Scan announce, sounding 'disjointed and difficult to follow.' The [SC2-PROBE] caught only ONE SpeakChannel2 call (caller=0x6DC8E281, the scan_tts.cpp auto-announce path), so the second voice MUST be coming through ScreenReader::Speak() — which routes to s_pVoice (voice 1) and was NOT being probed. Aaron noted he'd thought we had disabled the dual-channel audio system, but s_pVoice2 was still being created unconditionally in InitSAPI. Two changes: (1) InitSAPI now leaves s_pVoice2 = nullptr and skips its SpMMAudioOut allocation. SpeakChannel2 falls back to s_pVoice (voice 1) so all speech goes through one voice and SAPI's per-voice queue serializes everything — no simultaneous overlap is mechanically possible. Trade-off: SpeakChannel2(text, interrupt=true) now purges voice 1's queue, potentially cutting off menu/command speech mid-utterance. Aaron has confirmed he prefers this over overlapping speech. (2) Added SC1-PROBE in ScreenReader::Speak(const wchar_t*, bool) mirroring the SC2-PROBE pattern: logs caller _ReturnAddress() and 75-char text preview as [SC1-PROBE] in ff8_mod.log every call. With single-channel mode active, simultaneous overlap can't occur, but the SC1-PROBE will tell us WHICH path was redundantly speaking the scan announce on voice 1 (and let us fix the duplicate at its source in v0.14.62). Files: screen_reader.cpp (skip voice 2 creation + SC1-PROBE in Speak), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). Strip both probes in v0.14.62+ once root cause is found. Note that v0.14.60's architectural fix (announce trigger on sub_B687C0 first-fire) IS still in place and working correctly (single SC2-PROBE confirmed the auto-announce content is correct); v0.14.61 just prevents whatever is calling Speak() with the same/related text from doubling up audibly.

// v0.14.60: Architectural fix for the v0.14.59 duplicate-announce bug + cosmetic + diagnostic. v0.14.59 BAT proved the architecture fired the auto-announce at action-commit time (when the kind=0x06 val=50 popup spawns), about 9 seconds before the actual Scan UI window opens visually. Aaron heard the announce TWICE: once at start of cast animation (full content), once when the window appeared (just name+description). The mod log AudioDucker showed two distinct duck windows (19:36:33-39 and 19:36:42-47); the second has no corresponding [SCAN-TTS] Auto-announce log line, so its source is unknown as of v0.14.59 BAT review. v0.14.60 architecture: (1) MOVED announce trigger from popup-spawn (SPRITE-POLL NEW kind=0x06 val=50 in screenshot.inl) to FIRST FIRE of sub_B687C0 hook in scan_tts.cpp — sub_B687C0 fires when the engine actually reads scan text for the UI render, matching what the player sees on screen. (2) Per-scan reset of s_scanHookFireCount in OnScanCast(_, true) so 'first fire' means 'first fire of THIS scan event,' not the cumulative count across all scans this battle. HookedScanGetText now calls OnScanPopupSpawn on count==1 (instead of being a vestigial no-op); subsequent fires are throttled diagnostic-only. (3) screenshot.inl SPRITE-POLL NEW path no longer calls OnScanPopupSpawn; the comment explains the move. The DESPAWN edge still calls OnScanPopupDespawn because the popup record stays alive for the full UI session and only despawns when the player dismisses the window. (4) Cosmetic: BuildAutoAnnounce now strips trailing periods (and trailing whitespace) from the description before appending its own period, fixing the 'may be a shark.. Press number keys' double-period in v0.14.59 BAT. (5) Diagnostic: ScreenReader::SpeakChannel2(const wchar_t*) logs every call with caller _ReturnAddress() and a 75-char text preview as [SC2-PROBE] in ff8_mod.log. If v0.14.60 BAT still shows two AudioDucker duck windows during a Scan, the SC2-PROBE log lines will identify the second caller's return address (which can be mapped back to source by subtracting the dinput8.dll load base). Strip the probe in v0.14.61+ once the second caller is identified or the duplicate disappears. Files: scan_tts.cpp (architecture + cosmetic), battle_tts_screenshot.inl (remove popup-spawn trigger), screen_reader.cpp (SC2-PROBE), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The magicId==39 fallback in ewm.inl is kept as defense-in-depth — in the new architecture it just re-arms the snapshot/counter (idempotent if popup-hook path beat it) and the next sub_B687C0 fire announces. Doomtrain Scan-effect remains a known limitation if it doesn't trigger sub_48D200 popup hook AND doesn't trigger sub_B687C0 — not yet observed.

// v0.14.59: Scan UX redesign — silent action-layer + screen-open auto-announce + interactive number-key queries 1..0 (1..4 wired this build, 5..0 stub 'Not implemented yet.' for v0.14.60..63 chapter). The action-layer (popup hook in noeffect.inl, magicId==39 in ewm.inl) silently captures a per-slot ScanSnapshot {name, level, HP, monster_id, description} and sets s_pendingScanSlot — NO speech. One frame later, the [SPRITE-POLL] NEW emitter in screenshot.inl detects the kind=0x06 val=50 popup record and calls ScanTTS::OnScanPopupSpawn, which speaks '<Name>. <Description>. Press number keys 1 through 0 for details.' on Channel 2 and sets s_scanScreenActiveSlot. While the screen is active, PollHPCheckKeys in battle_tts_hp.inl routes 1..0 to ScanTTS::SpeakField for live querying. On popup despawn, OnScanPopupDespawn clears s_scanScreenActiveSlot. v0.14.60 supersedes the announce-trigger location (see above).

// v0.14.57: Suppress duplicate Scan announcements. v0.14.56 BAT confirmed the spurious 'No effect on <target>' is gone, but Aaron heard the Scan announcement TWICE per cast: once at action-commit (popup-based action-layer) and once 10 seconds later when the Scan UI opened and triggered the dispatcher hook. Fix: two-tier dedup. Added `bool fromActionLayer = false` parameter to OnScanCast. The two action-layer callers (popup path in noeffect.inl, magicId==39 path in ewm.inl) pass true. When OnScanCast announces with fromActionLayer=true, it sets a (slot, tick) lock for 30 seconds. Hook callers (HookedScanDispatch + HookedScanGetText, both default false) check the lock at function entry. Lock is per-slot; bypassed by action-layer. BAT PASS 2026-04-29 23:38: three Scans → three announcements. Five files: scan_tts.h, scan_tts.cpp, battle_tts.cpp, battle_tts_ewm.inl, battle_tts_noeffect.inl.

// v0.14.56: Fix v0.14.55 LNK2019 by removing the duplicate ScanTTS forward declaration from battle_tts_noeffect.inl. Cross-namespace forward decls inside .inl files create nested-namespace symbols (BattleTTS::ScanTTS::OnScanCast) different from the global ::ScanTTS::OnScanCast. Fix: removed the redundant forward decl. The global declaration already exists in battle_tts.cpp at file scope.

// v0.14.55: Action-layer Scan detection via the existing popup hook (HookedPopupSprite, sub_48D200). v0.14.54 BAT proved that BOTH sub_B687C0 and sub_84F860 only fire for the FULL Scan view (first scan of a target in a battle): the dispatcher counter reached only ~120-179 fires total despite Aaron casting Scan multiple times — i.e. only ONE Scan UI session triggered hooks. The compacted/reduced view shown on repeat Scans skips BOTH window-rendering paths entirely. Fix: detect Scan one level above the UI — at the action-commit popup. Every Scan cast (regardless of view path) spawns the spell-name popup with text_id=0x06 and value=0x32 (spell ID 50 = Scan) just before sub_48E830's snapshot recorder runs. battle_tts_sprite.inl now writes `s_lastScanCastTick` whenever that popup fires; battle_tts_noeffect.inl's NoEffect_RecordSnapshot reads it at the top: if a Scan-cast tick is within 1000 ms, the snapshot is skipped (no spurious 'No effect') and ScanTTS::OnScanCast(targetSlot) is called directly to announce. OnScanCast's existing (slot, ~2 s quiet window) dedup ensures the new path doesn't double-announce when the v0.14.54 dispatcher hook also fires on full views. The dispatcher and text-fetch hooks remain in place as defense-in-depth and to provide independent detection paths. Three files touched: battle_tts_sprite.inl (s_lastScanCastTick write site in HookedPopupSprite), battle_tts_noeffect.inl (forward decl + read + skip in NoEffect_RecordSnapshot), ff8_accessibility.h (version). v0.14.55 BUILD FAILED with LNK2019 — namespace mistake on the forward declaration. v0.14.56 fixes it.

// v0.14.54: Second Scan-detection hook on sub_84F860 (the Scan UI phase dispatcher at 0x0084F860) to catch the compacted view shown on repeat Scans of the same target in a battle. v0.14.53 BAT (with the diagnostic logging) proved the v0.14.52 sub_B687C0 hook only catches the FULL Scan view: `[SCAN-HOOK] fire #1 slot=3` appeared once at 21:38:14 for the first Bite Bug cast, then never again — the second Bite Bug Scan at ~21:38:38 (compacted view, same target) skipped sub_B687C0 entirely, leading to a spurious `[NOEFFECT-FLUSH] No effect on Bite Bug` at 21:38:48. Aaron's hint confirmed: FF8 shows a compacted/reduced-info view when scanning the same target a second+ time in a battle, and that view doesn't fetch the description text. Fix: hook sub_84F860 in addition to sub_B687C0. v0.14.55 BAT showed THIS hook also doesn't fire for compacted view, requiring the action-layer fix above. The dual hooks remain installed for defense-in-depth and full-view detection.

// v0.14.53: Diagnostic build to find why Magic-menu Scan still triggers spurious 'No effect on <target>' under v0.14.52. v0.14.52 BAT installed the sub_B687C0 hook cleanly (trampoline at 0x13160F60, log line confirms 'OK') but the Magic-menu Scan on Bite Bug 1 at 21:22:17 still produced 'No effect on Bite Bug 1' — no [SCAN-TTS] Cast line between the watchdog snapshot and the flush. Two possibilities: (a) hook never fires for Magic-menu Scan because that path uses a different rendering function, OR (b) hook fires but OnScanCast silently dedups. This build adds logging to disambiguate: HookedScanGetText logs the first call and every 60th thereafter as `[SCAN-HOOK] sub_B687C0 fire #N slot=N`, and OnScanCast logs `[SCAN-DEDUP] OnScanCast suppressed slot=N` when dedup blocks. After BAT, if [SCAN-HOOK] lines appear during Magic-menu Scan, we know the hook fires and the issue is dedup; if none appear, the hook never fires for that path and we need a different signal (likely hook a different function in the Scan UI rendering chain). One file: scan_tts.cpp (diagnostic instrumentation only — no behavior change beyond logging).

// v0.14.52: Path-agnostic Scan detection via MinHook on scan_get_text_sub_B687C0 (0x00B687C0). v0.14.51 BAT proved the magicId==39 polling detection in PollBattleMagicId only fires for Draw-Cast Scans because Magic-menu Scan goes through a different effect dispatcher that doesn't write to battle_magic_id at 0x01D99A68. Aaron cast Scan 6 times (3 enemy via Draw-Cast, 3 ally via Magic menu); only the Draw-Cast cases produced [SCAN-TTS] Cast lines and worked. The five Magic-menu Scans (one enemy at 19:54:53 + three party + one missed) each got the spurious 'No effect on <target>' watchdog announcement because OnScanCast (and therefore the watchdog cancel) never fired. Fix: hook scan_get_text_sub_B687C0 — the FFNx-named Scan-text-fetch function the engine calls every render frame the Scan UI is open. It fires for ALL Scan casts regardless of cast path because every Scan UI rendering goes through it. Slot is arg[0]. Added MinHook include + hook scaffold + InstallScanGetTextHook to scan_tts.cpp; called from ScanTTS::Initialize(). Added (slot, ~2 s quiet window) dedup to OnScanCast so the per-frame hook fires (60+ Hz during UI open) only announce once per cast: announces on slot change OR after a quiet gap; same-slot fires within the gap are suppressed. The magicId==39 path in PollBattleMagicId is retained as a parallel detection — dedup makes it a no-op when the hook fired first. Two files: scan_tts.cpp (hook + dedup), ff8_accessibility.h (version).

// v0.14.51: Scan TTS spurious 'No effect on <target>' suppression. v0.14.50 BAT proved Scan announcement worked correctly ('Glacial Eye. Level 9. HP 265 of 265.' at 19:14:37) but at 19:14:42 the no-effect watchdog from battle_tts_noeffect.inl queued 'No effect on Glacial Eye.' and flushed it 10 s later — same root cause as the v0.14.49 Draw-Stock bug: Scan goes through the player-magic action-announce path (sub_48E830 retaddr=0x0048594E, arg[1]=0x16) which records a watchdog snapshot, but Scan deals no HP / status / display change BY DESIGN (it just opens an info window). Fix: new public BattleTTS::CancelNoEffectWatchdogForSlot(int) wrapping a static NoEffect_CancelForSlot helper in battle_tts_noeffect.inl that clears both s_pendingSpellNoEffect (snapshot stage) and s_pendingNoEffectAnnounce (already-queued stage) for the given slot. ScanTTS::OnScanCast calls it after SpeakChannel2 so the genuine Scan announcement fires and the watchdog stays silent. Timing analysis: PollBattleMagicId (where ScanTTS detection runs) executes BEFORE PollPendingSpellNoEffect in the same Update tick, so the cancel is always applied before the watchdog could ever expire. The defensive queued-announcement clear covers the corner case where effect 39 is written several ticks after the snapshot. Three files touched: battle_tts_noeffect.inl (new helper), battle_tts.h + battle_tts.cpp (public wrapper), scan_tts.cpp (call site + Initialize log update).

// v0.14.50: Scan spell TTS — first slice. Detection in PollBattleMagicId (battle_tts_ewm.inl): when battle_magic_id transitions to 39 (FF8BattleEffect::Scan, confirmed via FFNx canary), read the active battle target bitmask at 0x01D76884, resolve to slot via BitmaskToSlot, and announce '<Name>. Level <L>. HP <cur> of <max>.' on Channel 2 via BattleSpeakEvent. Soft fallback: when maxHP > 99,999, announce 'HP unknown' instead — wiki guidance for hidden-HP enemies. Exact engine mechanism (whitelist vs numeric) deferred to v0.14.53; for now any max > 99,999 hides the value. No new files; ~30 lines added inside PollBattleMagicId reusing existing helpers (GetSlotName, BitmaskToSlot, BattleSpeakEvent) and entity-struct offset constants. Description, elemental affinities, status resists, and repeat-spam suppression are upcoming slices v0.14.51..v0.14.53. Deep research findings at Plan & Research Documents/Scan spell deep research results.md.

// v0.14.49: Draw-Stock no-effect suppression. The watchdog in battle_tts_noeffect.inl was firing 'No effect on <enemy>' ~6 s after every Draw > Target > Spell > Stock action because Stock genuinely doesn't change HP, status, or any of the watchdog's monitored signals. Draw > Cast must keep the watchdog active (a Fire-absorbing enemy castled with Fire is a genuine no-effect case that should announce). Fix: at the top of NoEffect_RecordSnapshot, gate on s_submenuCommandId == 0x16 (Draw command was selected during this turn's submenu nav) AND the live byte at 0x01D768D9 == 0 (Stock cursor, not Cast). Both conditions must be true to skip the snapshot. s_submenuCommandId persists across the turn-end -> action-execute transition because PollTurnAndCommands only resets it on the NEXT turn's start, so it's still valid when sub_48E830 fires for the Draw action. We don't add s_inSubmenu to the gate because that flag IS cleared on activeChar -> 0xFF before sub_48E830 fires. The live byte at 0x01D768D9 is read fresh every time rather than falling back on s_drawStockCastPrev (which could be 0xFF if the player confirmed Stock at default cursor without ever moving). One-file change in src/battle_tts_noeffect.inl plus this version bump.

// v0.14.48: Audio ducking tuning pass after v0.14.47 BAT. Three changes per Aaron's listening: (1) hold time 200ms -> 800ms on both buses to bridge gaps between menu-nav announcements (the v0.14.47 200ms hold was just short enough that volume bounced up between successive menu cursor moves). (2) BGM depth -15 dB -> -10 dB; v0.14.47 BGM duck was deeper than necessary now that ducking is working at all. (3) SFX depth -6 dB -> -15 dB; attack animation SFX still masked TTS at -6 dB, so escalated to match the depth that was previously on BGM. Pure constant changes in GameAudio::Initialize -- no architecture changes.

// v0.14.47: Audio ducking Phase 2 — reusable AudioDucker module with per-bus configuration (BGM and SFX), reference-counted BeginDuck/EndDuck trigger, smoothed one-pole envelope (separate attack/release time constants), hold timer to bridge SAPI chunk gaps, and dB-based config converted to linear gain internally. New files: src/audio_ducker.h + audio_ducker.cpp. Wired into game_audio.cpp via four function-pointer accessors (GetUserBgmVolume / ApplyBgmVolume / GetUserSfxVolume / ApplySfxVolume) so the ducker stays domain-agnostic. GameAudio::Update polls ScreenReader::IsSpeaking() once per tick on the rising/falling edge to drive BeginDuck / EndDuck; existing 500ms periodic Reapply gates on !AudioDucker::IsActive(); F2 toggle now actually switches ducking on/off via AudioDucker::SetEnabled. Default depths: BGM -15 dB (deep — GF summon music masks AD heavily), SFX -6 dB (light — most SFX short). Default times: 100 ms attack, 600 ms release, 200 ms hold. dB depths hardcoded for now because Config::GetInt uses GetPrivateProfileInt which doesn't handle negative-value INI keys cleanly — tunable in source. F5/F6/F7/F8 volume changes skip the immediate Reapply call when ducker is active so they don't briefly flash to user-level mid-duck (ducker picks up the new user vol on next Tick).

// v0.14.45: SFX volume control + audio-ducking toggle + full keyboard layout reshuffle. Resolved game-side sfx_set_master_volume via opcode_effectplay2 chain (pExecuteOpcodeTable[0x21] -> +0x5F sfx_play_to_current_playing_channel -> +0x35 play_sfx_on_channel -> +0xA1 sfx_set_volume; sfx_get_master_volume = sfx_set_volume - 0x10; sfx_set_master_volume = sfx_get_master_volume - 0xE0). FFNx replaces sfx_set_master_volume with a JMP to its own setSFXMasterVolume bridge in the same way it replaces set_midi_volume. Mirrored the v0.09.24 BGM hook architecture: scan the FFNx replacement bytes for nxAudioEngine ptr + setSFXMasterVolume method address, MinHook the FFNx function, periodically reapply via direct __fastcall thiscall shim. New persistent INI keys: sfx_volume (default 100), tts_duck_enabled (default 1), sfx_duck_ratio (default 30, percent for hand-editability). Auto-duck logic itself is deferred to v0.14.46 — F2 toggle persists the bool and announces state but is otherwise inert until Phase 2 wires ScreenReader::IsSpeaking() polling. Keyboard layout reshuffled per Aaron: F1 cycle voice, F2 ducking toggle, F3/F4 speech rate, Shift+F3/F4 speech volume, F5/F6 SFX volume, F7/F8 BGM volume. Removed v0.12.21 stale F2 'Director Varblock' diagnostic block from field_nav_handlekeys.inl (DEVNOTES called it unused but it was firing). Generalized 'Function key repurposing rule' added to DEVNOTES.

// v0.14.44: GF summon audio descriptions. New module gf_audio_desc.cpp/h mirrors fmv_audio_desc architecture. Per-GF VTT files embedded as RCDATA resources (IDR_VTT_GF_BASE = 6000). Trigger wired into PollBattleMagicId() in battle_tts_ewm.inl: when battle_magic_id changes to a GF effect ID, GfAudioDesc::OnGFAnimationStart fires and begins cue playback on Channel 2 (event voice). Stops playback when battle_magic_id reverts (covers natural end + R1+L1 skip). VTTs cover all 16 junctioned GFs (Quezacotl, Shiva, Ifrit, Siren, Brothers, Diablos, Carbuncle, Leviathan, Pandemona, Cerberus, Alexander, Doomtrain, Bahamut, Cactuar, Tonberry, Eden) plus Phoenix (item) and Odin (auto-summon). Each opens with GF name + attack name (replaces visual popup), then cues describe animation phases. Timing draws on community references; will need BAT tuning per GF since animation durations are approximate.

// v0.14.43: Diagnostic cleanup post-Bug-A resolution. Stripped DumpItemMenuState (the [ITEM-DUMP] block) from src/battle_tts_menu.inl — v0.14.42 BAT verified the disassembly model so the dump is no longer needed. Stripped [BATTLESPEAK-DIAG] log preamble/postamble from BattleSpeak in src/battle_tts.cpp — added in v0.14.37 to investigate items audio purge, no longer needed. Stripped [SPEAK-DIAG] log statements from ScreenReader::Speak in src/screen_reader.cpp — same provenance. Net: ~150 lines of diagnostic code removed; F12 stays free for next investigation. The [ITEM-LIST] block is retained (low overhead, one shot per submenu open, useful for future regression checks). No functional changes; behavior identical to v0.14.42 with cleaner logs.

// v0.14.40: Items submenu — visual page/slot fix + comprehensive diagnostic dump. v0.14.39 BAT showed (a) FindItemControllerNode() returns NULL on every cursor read (40/40) — the v0.10.104 handler signature 0x4F81F0 isn't where expected in this build, OR FFNx replaced it, OR the pool layout shifted; (b) Aaron's saved arrangement has gaps between items ("Elixir was page 1 slot 4 when really it was on page 3 following a bunch of empty slots"). Fix part 1 (visual position): added boIdx field to BattleItemEntry tracking the original battle_order position. BuildItemList now records boIdx for each item. New GetItemVisualPos() helper computes page/slot from boIdx (so saved-arrangement gaps are reflected). Both items announce paths (main + SUBMENU-DELAYED) use it. Theory: BATTLE_SUBMENU_CURSOR is a compacted item-index cursor; engine renders items at their battle_order POSITIONS with empties preserved. Fix part 2 (diagnostic): added DumpItemMenuState() that runs once per items submenu open. Logs raw 32 bytes of battle_order, inv lookup for each non-FF entry, hex dump of all 10 pool slots first 0x40 bytes, every code-pointer DWORD found in pool memory (0x004XXXXX or 0x6E70XXXX range), explicit search for ITEM_HANDLER (0x4F81F0) at every offset, and 32 bytes around BATTLE_SUBMENU_CURSOR for cursor-neighborhood inspection.

// v0.14.39: Bug A items-ordering attempted fix via pool-node helper. BAT proved helper returns NULL across the entire battle — handler offset/address don't match this build.

// v0.14.38: Bug B fully FIXED (every damage event fired via sub_5068B0 / Flag #2). Bug A items-not-heard FIXED (false-exit suppression extended to Item submenu). Bug A new variant discovered: items announce in wrong order. Three flags codified: Flag #1 (sub_48EF80) = action-launch, NOT a trigger. Flag #2 (sub_5068B0) = damage sprite, PRIMARY. Flag #3 (anim flag 1->0) = catch-all fallback.

// v0.14.37: Bug B revert + Bug A diagnostic. v0.14.36 BAT FAILED — popup-create primary fired 1-5s before render-hook. Reverted to render-hook primary, popup-create as fallback. Added [BATTLESPEAK-DIAG] in BattleSpeak and [SPEAK-DIAG] in ScreenReader::Speak for items submenu investigation. Diagnostic logs remain in v0.14.38 and will be removed in v0.14.39.

